# Line endings.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` on a Windows checkout hands CRLF to everything that reads the working tree.
# That is normally harmless — but the AI-DLC scripts in `.specsmd/` parse artifact frontmatter with
# an LF-only regex:
#
#     content.match(/^---\n([\s\S]+?)\n---/)
#
# On a CRLF file that match fails, `extractFrontmatter` returns null, and the caller **skips the
# file without saying so**. `status-integrity.cjs` then reports "0 inconsistencies" over artifacts
# it never opened: on 2026-08-17 it was scanning 7 of 17 bolts and calling the result clean, which
# is how five complete bolts kept stories marked `ready` with nobody noticing.
#
# Pinning Markdown to LF in the *working tree* is half the fix. The other half is the regex itself,
# which is now `/^---\r?\n([\s\S]+?)\r?\n---/` in all three scripts.
#
# Both halves are needed, and the reasoning for each is the other one's failure mode:
#
#   * The regex alone is fragile across a `.specsmd` upgrade, which would overwrite the patch.
#   * **These attributes alone do not work**, and that was discovered the hard way on 2026-08-17.
#     They govern what git *writes on checkout*; the scripts read whatever is on disk *now*. Any
#     tool that rewrites a file in between can reintroduce CRLF — Python's `write_text` does it
#     silently on Windows, which is exactly how six files reverted within hours of the original
#     normalisation, dropping the scan from 24 bolts to 17 with no warning.
#
# So: attributes to survive the upgrade, regex to survive the next tool. If you find yourself here
# because the scan count looks low, check for CRLF in the working copy first.
*.md text eol=lf

# The memory bank is the artifact hierarchy those scripts walk. Everything in it, whatever the
# extension, is read by a tool at some point.
memory-bank/** text eol=lf
.specsmd/** text eol=lf
